====Verb====
{{en-verb|rids|ridding|rid|past2=ridded|past_ptc2=ridded|past_ptc3=ridden|past_ptc3_qual=rare, nonstandard}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[free]] (something) from a [[hindrance]] or [[annoyance]].
#: {{synonyms|en|deliver|disencumber}}
#: {{ux|en|I’m glad to be '''rid''' of that stupid nickname.}}
#: {{ux|en|We’re trying to '''rid''' the world of poverty.}}
#* '''1170''', King Henry II (offhand remark)
#*: Will no one '''rid''' me of this troublesome priest?
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=May|title=News and Comment: Minister hamstrings BR workshops|journal=Modern Railways|page=291|passage=If the Government believes that part of the railways' salvation is to be found in '''ridding''' them of extraneous concerns, it should have had the courage either to close the railway works down as quickly as possible, or to hive them off as an entirely separate concern, [...].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jacob Steinberg|title=Wigan shock Manchester City in FA Cup again to reach semi-finals|titleurl=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/mar/09/manchester-city-wigan-athletic-fa-cup|journal=The Guardian|date=9 March 2014|passage=All the billions in the world and Manchester City still cannot '''rid''' themselves of the most persistent thorn in their side.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|mostly|obsolete}} To [[banish]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=John H. Goodwin|title=The Reluctant Spy|page=293|passage=Worst of all were the leeches. The soldiers had managed to '''rid''' them from the camp interiors, but once you ventured out on patrol and into the wetlands, they were everywhere.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[kill]].

